# Stowed to ~/.stow-global-ignore, where GNU Stow actually reads it. A
# .stow-local-ignore is only honoured INSIDE the package being stowed, so the
# old copy at the repository root was silently ignored -- which is how
# ~/.DS_Store and ~/lefthook.yml ended up as symlinks into the fish package.
#
# This file REPLACES Stow's built-in default list, so the defaults are
# repeated here. Patterns are Perl regexes, anchored, matched against the
# basename (or against the package-relative path when they contain a slash).
#
# Stow only consults this file once it is linked into ~, so `stow stow` has to
# run (for real, not with -n) before any other package: until then every
# other package is filtered by the built-in list, which knows nothing about
# .DS_Store, lefthook.yml or .gitattributes, and the submodules' copies of
# those either conflict with each other or land in ~.
#
# Deliberately NOT listed: this file itself. Ignoring it made `stow -D stow`
# a silent no-op: the package's only file was skipped, so the symlink in ~
# was never removed.
#
# Stow's defaults
RCS
.+,v
CVS
\.\#.+
\.cvsignore
\.svn
_darcs
\.hg
\.git
\.gitignore
\.gitmodules
.+~
\#.*\#
^/README.*
^/LICENSE.*
^/COPYING
# Ours
\.DS_Store
# The submodules carry their own repo-level files at the package root: their
# lefthook config and, in emacs, a .gitattributes for the elpaca.lock diff
# driver. Git does not read ~/.gitattributes (the global file is
# core.attributesFile), so linking it into ~ is pure clutter.
lefthook\.yml
\.gitattributes
\.stow-local-ignore
